{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":44688,"ns":0,"title":"Almost everywhere","extract":"In measure theory (a branch of mathematical analysis), a property holds almost everywhere (sometimes abbreviated as a.e.) if the set for which the property does not hold is negligible in a technical sense. The notion of \"almost everywhere\" is a companion notion to the concept of measure zero, and is analogous to the notion of almost surely in probability theory.","description":"Everywhere except a set of measure zero","descriptionsource":"local"}]}}